@prefix assembly: <http://example.org/orexis/assembly#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/assembly#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/assembly> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Assembly" ;
    rdfs:comment """How a build is put together, and how its parts reach each other.

Merged FIRST, before the kernel's, because the kernel's own extension points are instances of a
class declared here. This is not a package — it is what finds packages — and neither is `agent/`;
a tree and its loader are not two trees (the-assembly-is-not-the-mind).""" .

:Extension a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Extension point" ;
    rdfs:comment "A named place another package may contribute to, filled by `@contributes(TERM)` on a function. Was `orexis:Hook`, and in the kernel. Declared by whoever owns the QUESTION — the kernel's for the BDI-shaped ones, a package's for its own — and refused if nobody declares it, because a term nobody declared would be answered by silence (a-hook-is-a-term). Two moments, one mechanism: a package module fills the assembly-time points and a Module instance fills the run-time ones, and which is which is decided by where the decorated function lives rather than by anything stated." .

:Service a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Service" ;
    rdfs:comment "Something one package OFFERS for others to use, reached by its term and never by import — a store, a sink, a client. Offered with `@provides(TERM)` in a manifest, needed with `@requires(TERM)` on a class, and resolved on first touch. Fan-in, where an Extension is fan-out: one thing looked up, rather than everyone's opinion collected. What distinguishes it from a capability is how it comes to exist — no premise grants it and no world can refuse one agent a service another has (see knowledge/domain/service.md)." .

:signature a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Extension ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "signature" ;
    rdfs:comment "The parameters a function filling this point must take, and what it returns. Published ON the point so a package can fill one another package declared WITHOUT importing whoever declared it — which is the difference between an extension point and a private callback. Checked strictly against every filler's parameter names: before this, a filler whose parameters did not match raised TypeError, which the asker logged as `could not answer` and stepped over, so a wrong signature was a module quietly not participating. A string rather than a structure, because it is read by people and compared by name." .

#################  The assembly-time points  #################
#
#  What a package brings to a build. Each is handed the package's OWN directory and nothing
#  else, and that argument is the whole of the phase discipline. It is not handed a world: the
#  T-Box is merged before any world is chosen, so there is none to give. It is not handed a
#  store: `refresh_public` runs before the belief base is open. A contribution cannot reach for
#  what does not exist yet, because the argument is not there — which is why no point declares
#  a phase and none needs to.
#
#  None of these carries `orexis:row`. A row says whether ANSWERING may block or search, which is a
#  fact about the cognitive layering and means nothing at assembly time: there is no planner to
#  stay out of and no latency to keep.
#
#  A contribution returns PATHS. Content — a string of Turtle, fetched or generated — is the
#  wider signature this wanted, and it is not implemented: some fifty consumers read these as
#  files (`.read_text()`, `.parent.name`, `file_iri()`), no package needs it, and the way in when
#  one does is to materialise content to a path at assembly rather than to widen fifty call
#  sites. Not a graph either: every `__init__` is imported at assembly, and rdflib in fourteen
#  knowledge-only packages is a cost with nothing to show for it.

:vocabulary a :Extension ;
    :signature "(package) -> list[Path]" ;
    rdfs:comment "What terms do you bring? Merged into the T-Box before anything reads a belief." .

:shapes a :Extension ;
    :signature "(package) -> list[Path]" ;
    rdfs:comment "What must be true of a thing that has what you bring? Handed to the validator." .

:derivation a :Extension ;
    :signature "(package) -> list[Path]" ;
    rdfs:comment "What premise grants your capability? SPARQL updates, run once in package order — and a premise may not rest on another package's conclusions." .

:wants a :Extension ;
    :signature "(package) -> list[Path]" ;
    rdfs:comment "What does an agent holding your capability therefore want? Run on every rebuild of the desire modality, never at genesis." .

:actions a :Extension ;
    :signature "(package) -> list[Path]" ;
    rdfs:comment "What ways of acting do you bring? One `orexis:Action` node each: precondition, effect, and the capability that takes it." .

:review a :Extension ;
    :signature "(package) -> list[Path]" ;
    rdfs:comment "What would you like an agent to reconsider about itself? A SELECT binding ?term and ?value, never an update and never Python." .
